  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1050 PM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nOur weather pattern this week will be dominated by a persistent \nupper level ridge over the western United States. This will keep our \nforecast area dry with above normal temperatures through Friday. By \nthis weekend, a weak system could bring light precipitation (30-40% \nchance as of now) to the high country and cooler temperatures to the \nregion.\n\nCurrent water vapor satellite imagery shows a weak upper level \nshortwave over the far Pacific Northwest. This will trek across the \nnorthern United States and into the northern Great Plains Tuesday \nand into Wednesday. While this system will be too far away from \nColorado for any precipitation chances, there will be increased \ncloud cover over our region on Tuesday, which should keep \ntemperatures over the Denver area a few degrees cooler than \nMonday. However, still expect maximum temperatures to be in the \nmid-to-high 60s across the plains. A \"cool\" front (if you can \neven call it that) associated with the system will bring breezy \nwinds to the northern and eastern plains, with gusts up to 20-30 \nmph possible in the afternoon. Due to this, elevated fire weather \nconditions will be possible. However, relative humidity values \nshould stay above critical thresholds, especially with the \nexpected cloud cover. \n\nWe will be a bit cooler on Wednesday, with highs in the low-to-mid \n60s. But, this will be short-lived as the axis of the upper level \nridge should shift over wester Colorado on Thursday and Friday. \nTemperatures in the mid-to-high 60s will return, with a few ensemble \nsolutions showing low 70s.  \n\nEnsemble guidance does favor a weak system moving through the region \nover the weekend. This will give us our first chance in a while for \nmountain rain/snow showers. Accumulations look light at this point, \ngiven the lack of decent moisture and strong forcing. For the \nplains, precipitation chances do not look as favorable over the \nweekend. However, there could be an isolated rain shower or two. We \nshould begin cooling down towards the beginning of next week, with \nseasonal temperatures returning and possibly a more active \npattern. Stay tuned...",
